22:53:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The sensation of clogged ears is caused by a pressure imbalance between the middle ear (behind the eardrum) and the outside environment. Your middle ear is normally sealed off from the outside world by a small passage called the Eustachian tube, which only opens briefly when you swallow, yawn, or chew—allowing air to flow in or out to equalize pressure. When the outside air pressure changes too quickly for the Eustachian tube to keep up, a pressure difference builds, the eardrum can’t vibrate freely, and you feel that “clogged” or muffled sensation. This happens most dramatically during **rapid, large changes in altitude** — exactly what you experience in a plane or driving up and down steep mountain roads. There, the pressure can shift fast enough and over a long enough period that your normal swallowing just isn’t sufficient to equalize it. The discomfort often becomes worse on **descent**, because when outside pressure rises, the Eustachian tube tends to lock shut more easily, making the imbalance harder to clear. So why doesn’t a fast elevator in a skyscraper cause the same problem? It boils down to two factors: 1. **Total pressure change is much smaller.** A commercial airplane cabin is typically pressurized to an equivalent altitude of around 6,000–8,000 feet, while the ground altitude might be sea level. That’s a pressure swing equivalent to thousands of feet in a matter of minutes. Similarly, a drive over a mountain pass might involve a change of 5,000 feet or more. Even the tallest building in the world (Burj Khalifa, ~2,700 feet) represents only a fraction of that total altitude change. The absolute pressure difference your ears have to overcome is much less. 2. **The ride is too short for the imbalance to become noticeable.** While a high-speed elevator can ascend at over 1,000 feet per minute—a rate comparable to a plane’s climb—the trip itself is over in 10–60 seconds. Your natural swallowing reflex or a slight jaw movement can usually open the tube and equalize that small, brief pressure difference before you even notice it. In a car or plane, you’re exposed to sustained, accumulating pressure changes for many minutes, giving the imbalance time to build. Of course, if you have a cold or allergies that partially block the Eustachian tube, you *can* feel ear pressure even in an elevator. But under normal conditions, the combination of a small absolute pressure swing and a short duration is enough to keep the clogging sensation at bay.
